<Legal
  title="Accessibility"
  description="The accessibility standard DBM System's website and documentation aim for, what is checked and what is not, and how to report a problem."
  updated="16 November 2026"
>
  <p>
    We want everyone to be able to use this website and the documentation in Storybook, whatever they use to read it. This
    page says what we aim for, what we check and what we have not checked, and how to tell us about a problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Our aim</h2>
  <p>
    We aim for <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines">WCAG</abbr> 2.2 level AA on the website, in the
    documentation and in every component. This is our own assessment: it has not been audited by an independent body, so
    treat it as a target we work to and measure, not a certificate.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A "Skip to content" link is the first thing a keyboard reaches on each page.</li>
    <li>
      Everything can be used with a keyboard alone: the menus, search (open it with its button, or with ⌘K or Ctrl K), the
      mode toggle and the menu panel on a small screen. Focus is always visible, and a menu or panel returns focus to where
      it came from when it closes.
    </li>
    <li>Text and controls are designed to meet the WCAG contrast ratios in both light and dark mode.</li>
    <li>
      Light mode is the default, and the toggle in the header switches to dark. Neither relies on colour alone to show
      meaning.
    </li>
    <li>Animation is reduced or removed when your device asks for reduced motion.</li>
    <li>Pages are built for phone, tablet and desktop widths.</li>
    <li>Every button, link and field has a name that matches what you see, so voice control works as you would expect.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we check</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every page of this site is scanned with an automated accessibility tool (axe) on each change, in light and dark mode,
      with and without reduced motion. A violation of WCAG 2.2 level A or AA stops the change from being published.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every example in the documentation is run through the same tool in a real browser, and each interactive component is
      tested for its keyboard and focus behaviour.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each component is also reviewed against a written checklist before it is called finished, and pages are checked by
      hand with the keyboard alone, at phone, tablet and desktop widths, and in light and dark mode.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we have not checked</h2>
  <p>
    Automated tools find only part of what can go wrong. We have not yet tested with a range of screen readers, on every
    browser and real device, or in Windows high-contrast mode on a real machine (we emulate forced-colours mode in a
    browser). Some of the components in the documentation also have measured contrast values below the target, for
    example the faint track of a slider. Where that is so, it is listed, with the reason, in the
    <a href={accessibility.href}>Accessibility guide</a>, which also covers what every component guarantees and what stays
    your job when you build with them.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported browsers</h2>
  <p>
    The last two versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on desktop and mobile. Internet Explorer is not supported.
  </p>

  <h2>Tell us about a problem</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is hard to use with your setup, please
    <a href="https://github.com/designsbymason/dbm-system/issues">open an issue on GitHub</a> and tell us the page, what you
    were trying to do, and which browser and assistive technology you use. We treat accessibility problems as bugs and will
    say what we can do about yours. Issues are public, so please keep personal information out of them.
  </p>

  <h2>This statement</h2>
  <p>
    We wrote this statement on the date at the top, from our own checks. We will change it when what is true of the site
    changes.
  </p>
</Legal>
